# CURATED signatures — hand-written, not generated.
#
# Every signature here describes a type tony-codegen cannot describe correctly today. They are
# kept in this file rather than mixed into the generated ones because a curated signature can
# drift from the code and a generated one cannot, so the two need different upkeep — a test
# holds this set equal to the types declaring `codec=custom` (schema/curated_test.go).
#
# That difference is OURS, not the reader's. These documents are FOLDED with the generated ones
# into a single schema (see the package doc), which says nothing about where any definition came
# from. It used to publish a `curated:` list beside the shapes, which asked every caller to hold
# a distinction they cannot act on.
#
# These describe I/O SHAPES, which is what tony-codegen generates and what the assembled schema
# defines. A tony schema can also validate, through an `accept:` clause, and none of these carry
# one — generated or curated. That is not a gap to close here: codegen's business is marshalling
# and unmarshalling, and an acceptance criterion is a separate thing to author deliberately if a
# consumer ever needs one.
#
# It does mean a wrong `define` is wrong as DOCUMENTATION rather than as enforcement —
# nothing is rejected because of it. Which is exactly why it is worth correcting: describing
# the wire is the whole value of publishing these.
#
# Remove an entry as soon as codegen can produce it, and the endpoint will serve the
# generated one instead. That has already happened once: verse-entity and verse-event were
# curated because the schema mapper did not consult TextMarshaler and called entity.Ref an
# int; go-tony v0.0.93 fixed it, so they are generated now and their entries are gone.

# THE CHARTER GRAMMAR — four types with hand-written codecs (`codec=custom`), which is the
# declaration that the wire shape is NOT the Go shape. codegen derives from the Go shape
# regardless, so what it produces for them is either empty (a named map: trigger.Match) or,
# worse, a confident description of fields the codec never emits (a struct: Formula,
# Requirement). Both are corrected here.
#
# trigger.Match — the selector grammar, and the most-authored type in the system. On the wire
# it is EITHER an address string ("demo:pr:*") OR the object of dimensions below; the codec
# emits the string form whenever the match carries only system/kind/id. The dimension values
# are open because each may be a literal or a tony match operator (`id: !glob "hotfix-*"`),
# and `value` — the payload predicate — is open in SHAPE as well: a payload is any tony value,
# so a predicate over it may be a record, a scalar, a list, or an operator over one.
context:
- tony-format/context
- tony-base: github.com/signadot/tony-format/go-tony/schema
define:
  system: .[string]
  kind: .[string]
  id: .[tony-base:ir]
  op: .[tony-base:ir]
  value: .[tony-base:ir]
signature:
  name: verse-match
---
# trigger.Formula — the temporal layer. It emits a TAG, not an object: `!quorum 2`,
# `!until [...]`, `!and [...]`, `!or [...]`, `!not ...`, or a bare match atom when the formula
# is a single point-in-time match. codegen published it as `{args, op, req}` — the Go struct
# behind the codec, a shape that never appears on the wire. Empty is the honest `define` here:
# the form is a tagged union over the operator vocabulary, and the operators are tony's own
# (docs/trigger-model.md), not a set this signature can usefully restate.
context:
- tony-format/context
define: {}
signature:
  name: verse-formula
---
# trigger.Requirement — the payload of a formula's tag, e.g. the `2` in `!quorum 2` together
# with the facts it counts. Same correction as Formula: codegen described its Go fields
# (kind, n, choice, within, facts) and the codec emits none of them under those names.
context:
- tony-format/context
define: {}
signature:
  name: verse-requirement
---
# trigger.Condition — the one custom-codec type whose FIELD NAMES codegen got right; only the
# types were wrong. It emits `{subject: <match>, on: <formula>, when: <formula>}` — subject
# first, and always, since a condition without one is not installable — so `subject` points at
# the match signature, where codegen said `.[object(object)]` (an object of objects, which a
# `subject: demo:pr:*` string is not), while `on` and `when` point at the formula signature.
#
# `on` and `when` share that signature because they ARE one grammar: a predicate over the
# store, asked at two revisions for a cause and at one for a condition.
context:
- tony-format/context
define:
  on: .[nullable(verse-formula)]
  when: .[nullable(verse-formula)]
  subject: !verse-match null
signature:
  name: verse-condition
---
# trigger.Stratum — one level of the declared ordering. Two of its three fields codegen gets
# right; `match` is the correction. It is a UNION of match patterns, written bare when there
# is one and as a list when there are several, so the wire form is either a match or an array
# of them. codegen saw the Go slice and said `.[array(object)]`, which describes neither the
# bare form nor the address-string spelling a match may itself take.
#
# `level` orders and `name` addresses: the entity is trigger:strata:<name>, so two strata
# cannot share a name by construction, and sharing a LEVEL is refused at install because the
# number is the whole ordering.
context:
- tony-format/context
- tony-base: github.com/signadot/tony-format/go-tony/schema
define:
  name: .[string]
  level: .[int]
  match: .[tony-base:ir]
signature:
  name: verse-stratum
---
# entity.Doc — the verse's state at a path, and the one signature whose `define` is empty
# because the SHAPE is the answer rather than a record with fields. The document is
# `{system: {kind: {id: payload}}}`, so its keys are the systems a verse happens to hold, and a
# path into it answers with the subtree standing there: an object of kinds, of ids, a payload,
# or a place inside one. None of that is enumerable here.
#
# codec=custom for the same reason: the Go type wraps a node so the schema can NAME what a read
# answers with, and a generated codec would emit that wrapper as a field. The wire is the
# document itself, with nothing around it — which is the whole claim being made.
context:
- tony-format/context
define: {}
signature:
  name: verse-state
